---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ReviewedByCard from "../../components/ReviewedByCard.astro";
import { TRUST_PROFILES } from "../../config/trust";

const title = "15-Year vs 30-Year Mortgage Payment (Trade-offs)";
const description =
  "Compare monthly payment, total interest, and time horizon differences between 15-year and 30-year mortgages. Educational use only.";

const lastUpdated = "2026-04-05";
---
<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/guides/mortgage-payment-15-vs-30-year"
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
  authorProfile={TRUST_PROFILES.siteOwner}
  reviewProfiles={[TRUST_PROFILES.methodologyReview, TRUST_PROFILES.editorialReview]}
  robots="noindex, follow"
>
  <section class="card hero">
    <span class="pill">Guide</span>
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/guides">Guides</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">15 vs 30 year</span>
    </nav>
    <h1>15-year vs 30-year mortgage payment</h1>
    <p>
      A 15-year mortgage usually has a higher monthly payment but lower total interest. A 30-year mortgage lowers the payment
      but increases total interest over time. Use this guide to compare the trade-offs with consistent inputs.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/calculators/mortgage-payment-calculator">Mortgage payment calculator</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/calculators/amortization-schedule-calculator">Amortization schedule</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/mortgage-payment-total-cost-vs-payment">Payment vs total cost</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <ReviewedByCard
    writtenBy={TRUST_PROFILES.siteOwner}
    reviewedBy={TRUST_PROFILES.methodologyReview}
    secondaryReview={TRUST_PROFILES.editorialReview}
    reviewScope="Mortgage term-choice framing, DTI and affordability implications, and routing between mortgage payment, affordability, and total-cost comparison workflows."
    reviewedOn="2026-04-05"
  />

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Use this guide when term choice is the real mortgage-payment decision</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Use this page when the home and rate are already chosen, but you still need to decide whether the payment should be stretched over 15 years or 30 years.</li>
      <li>If your broader question is payment versus total interest across scenarios, move next to <a href="/guides/mortgage-payment-total-cost-vs-payment">mortgage payment vs total cost</a>.</li>
      <li>If the real blocker is whether the higher payment fits your budget, move to the <a href="/guides/mortgage-payment-affordability-checklist">mortgage payment affordability checklist</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Mortgage payment inputs to verify</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Loan amount, note rate, and term.</li>
      <li>Taxes, insurance, HOA, and PMI assumptions.</li>
      <li>Escrow rules or reserves used by the lender.</li>
      <li>Time horizon if comparing scenarios.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">How to compare fairly</h2>
    <ol class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Use the same loan amount and interest rate for both terms.</li>
      <li>Compare P&amp;I payment and total interest.</li>
      <li>Check DTI impact with the higher payment.</li>
      <li>Test your time horizon if you might move or refinance.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">What usually changes</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li><strong>Monthly P&amp;I:</strong> higher for 15-year, lower for 30-year.</li>
      <li><strong>Total interest:</strong> lower for 15-year because you repay faster.</li>
      <li><strong>Cash flow:</strong> 30-year leaves more monthly flexibility.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">DTI and affordability check</h2>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0">
      The 15-year payment can push the housing payment above DTI thresholds. Run the DTI calculator with the 15-year payment to see
      whether it keeps you within your target range.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Time horizon matters</h2>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0">
      If you expect to refinance or sell in a few years, compare total dollars paid over that window rather than the full term.
      The 15-year can still help, but the benefit depends on how long you keep the loan.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">How to think about flexibility versus payoff speed</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>A 15-year term forces the faster payoff through the required payment.</li>
      <li>A 30-year term preserves flexibility, but the lower required payment only helps if you intentionally use that flexibility well.</li>
      <li>This means the better choice depends as much on cash-flow resilience as on the math of total interest.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Comparing two offers with different rates or points.</li>
      <li>Ignoring taxes, insurance, HOA, and PMI in the total payment.</li>
      <li>Assuming you will always pay the 30-year like a 15-year without a plan.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="grid" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="card section prose col-6">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Related tools</h2>
      <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
        <li><a href="/calculators/mortgage-payment-calculator">Mortgage payment calculator</a></li>
        <li><a href="/calculators/amortization-schedule-calculator">Amortization schedule</a></li>
        <li><a href="/calculators/debt-to-income-calculator">DTI calculator</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card section prose col-6">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Related guides</h2>
      <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
        <li><a href="/guides/how-mortgage-payments-are-calculated">How mortgage payments are calculated</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/mortgage-payment-affordability-checklist">Affordability checklist</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/mortgage-payment-total-cost-vs-payment">Payment vs total cost</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/pay-off-mortgage-early-or-invest">Pay off early or invest?</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/refinance-break-even">Refinance break-even</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">FAQ</h2>
    <h3 style="margin:12px 0 0">Is a 15-year always cheaper?</h3>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:8px 0 0">
      It usually lowers total interest, but the higher payment can reduce flexibility. Compare with your budget and time horizon.
    </p>
    <h3 style="margin:12px 0 0">Can I take a 30-year and pay extra instead?</h3>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:8px 0 0">
      Yes, if your lender applies extras as principal-only. The key is whether you will consistently make those extra payments.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">References</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>
        CFPB:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/explore/home-loan-toolkit/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Your home loan toolkit
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        CFPB:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/mortgages/ready-to-buy-a-home/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Ready to buy a home?
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Next steps</h2>
    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:10px">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/calculators/mortgage-payment-calculator">Compare payments</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/calculators/extra-payment-calculator">Model extra payments</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/mortgage-payment-total-cost-vs-payment">Compare total cost</a>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:12px 0 0">Educational use only. Not financial advice.</p>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:12px 0 0; font-size:13px">Last updated: 2026-04-05</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>



